    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is my Ryobi battery not charging?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In order of likelihood: it is outside the safe temperature window and the charger has locked out, it has deep-discharged below the voltage at which charging is permitted, the contacts are dirty, the charger is faulty, or the pack has reached the end of its life. Let it sit indoors at room temperature for two hours and try again first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does a flashing red light on a Ryobi charger mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally a defective pack or a fault condition, as distinct from flashing red and green, which usually indicates a temperature problem. Patterns vary slightly between charger models, so check the label or manual. Before concluding the pack is dead, clean the contacts, let it reach room temperature and leave it on overnight."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you revive a dead Ryobi battery?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sometimes, if it is deep-discharged rather than damaged. Leave it on the charger overnight, since many chargers trickle a recovery current for hours before normal charging starts. What you should not do is jump it from another pack, which bypasses a safety lockout and can result in charging cells that are no longer safe."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
