          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The charger is not a dumb transformer. It monitors the pack and communicates through its lights, and learning to read those lights turns a mystery into a two-minute diagnosis.
        </p>

        <div id="blink-codes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Read the Blink Code First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Ryobi charger communicates its state through the LEDs, and the pattern distinguishes conditions that are temporary from ones that are terminal. The exact patterns vary a little between charger models, so check the label or the manual for yours, but the general scheme is consistent.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Indication</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Usually Means</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Action</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid green, no battery</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Powered and ready</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Insert pack</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flashing green</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Charging normally</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wait</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid green with battery</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Charge complete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flashing red and green</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Temperature out of range</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Let it reach room temperature</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid red</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deep discharged, attempting recovery</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leave it on for several hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flashing red</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Defective pack or fault</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">See the sections below</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">No lights at all</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No power, or charger fault</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check outlet and cord</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The distinction that matters most is between the temperature indication and the defective indication. A pack that is too hot or too cold is not faulty and will charge normally once it has equalised, and this accounts for a large share of the batteries people believe have died. It is the single most common false alarm.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other one worth knowing is that many chargers enter a slow recovery mode for a deeply discharged pack, trickling current at low rate before switching to normal charging. That can take several hours before anything appears to happen, and giving up after ten minutes is how recoverable packs get thrown away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work through the rest of this page in order. The sequence runs from most to least common cause, and most problems resolve in the first two sections.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Let the pack sit indoors at room temperature for two hours, then try again.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Too hot from heavy use, or too cold from a shed or a van, is the commonest reason a charger refuses a battery. The lockout is protection rather than a fault — charging lithium below freezing causes permanent damage — and it clears itself once the pack equalises.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Temperature Lockout
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lithium cells have a safe charging window, typically somewhere around 4 to 40 degrees Celsius, and chargers enforce it because charging outside that range causes real damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charging below freezing can plate metallic lithium onto the anode rather than intercalating it into the material properly. That is permanent capacity loss and, in the worst case, creates internal structures that can cause a short. The charger refusing a frozen pack is preventing that, and overriding it is not an option worth wanting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charging a very hot pack accelerates degradation and, at extremes, is a thermal runaway risk. A battery pulled straight off a chainsaw or a mower after hard work can easily be above the window, which is why chargers often pause on a pack that has just come off a demanding tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is patience, not intervention. Bring the pack indoors, put it somewhere at normal room temperature, and leave it for one to two hours — longer for a large pack from a genuinely cold shed, since thermal mass means the inside takes a while to catch up with the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not accelerate it. Never put a battery on a radiator, in an oven, in direct sun or under a heat gun, and equally do not put a hot pack in a freezer. Rapid temperature change is its own stressor and the extremes are dangerous. Ambient room temperature and time is the whole method.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a working habit, this is why storing packs indoors rather than in a shed or a vehicle matters, and why letting a pack cool for fifteen minutes after heavy use before charging is worth doing. Our guide on how long Ryobi batteries last covers the storage side properly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deep-discharge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Deep Discharge: The Common Killer
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the failure that actually ends packs, and it happens to batteries that were stored rather than used.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lithium cells must not be discharged below a minimum voltage. Below it, chemical changes begin that damage the cell permanently and can make recharging unsafe, so the pack's protection circuit locks out charging entirely once it detects that state. The pack is not empty in the ordinary sense; it is below the floor at which the electronics will allow current back in.
          </p>

          <p>
            How packs get there is almost always storage. A battery put away flat, or left with a small residual charge, slowly self-discharges over months until it crosses the threshold. A winter in a shed is a classic case, and so is a pack left in a tool that draws a tiny standby current.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some chargers will attempt recovery, applying a small current for an extended period to bring cell voltage back above the threshold, at which point normal charging resumes. That process can take several hours with little visible progress, so leave it on the charger overnight before concluding anything. A solid red indication often means exactly this is happening.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it does not recover after an overnight attempt, it generally will not. The chemistry has changed and the protection circuit is correctly refusing to charge a cell that is no longer safe to charge. That is the end of the pack, and it is worth accepting rather than pursuing further.
          </p>

          <p>
            The prevention is trivial and worth repeating: store packs at around half charge, indoors, and check them every three months. Almost every deep-discharge failure is a pack that was put away and forgotten, and five minutes twice a year prevents it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="contacts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Contacts and Connection
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mundane, easy to check, and responsible for a surprising share of charging problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect the terminals on both the pack and the charger. Sawdust, metal filings, corrosion and general workshop grime all interfere with the connection, and lithium packs communicate with the charger through additional contacts beyond the main power terminals, so a dirty data contact can cause a refusal even when power is connecting fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean them with a dry cloth or a cotton bud, and use isopropyl alcohol on anything stubborn. Do not use abrasives on the contacts, do not use water, and make sure everything is fully dry before inserting the pack. Compressed air is useful for clearing debris from the recesses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the pack seats properly and clicks home. A worn or damaged rail on either the pack or the charger can leave it sitting slightly proud, which can make intermittent contact that reads as a fault. Try the pack in a tool: if the tool runs normally, the pack's power terminals are fine and the problem is more likely the charger interface or a data contact.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cross-test if you can. Put a known-good battery in the suspect charger, and the suspect battery in a different charger. That single test separates a pack problem from a charger problem in under a minute and is worth doing before anything else if you have a second of either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check the obvious supply issues: the outlet works, the charger's cord is undamaged, and if it is on an extension lead or a switched socket, that the switch is on. A charger showing no lights at all is far more often a supply problem than a failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="revive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Revival Attempts, and Which Are Worth Trying
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is a lot of advice online about reviving dead lithium packs. Some of it is harmless, some is a genuine safety risk, and it is worth separating the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Worth trying: the overnight charge described above, cleaning the contacts, cross-testing with another charger, and letting the pack equalise to room temperature. These are safe, they address the actual common causes, and between them they recover most recoverable packs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also reasonable: inserting the pack into a tool briefly and then returning it to the charger, which on some models resets the communication between pack and charger. And simply trying again the next day, since a pack sitting at the margin sometimes accepts a charge on a second attempt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not worth doing: the widely circulated technique of jumping a dead pack from a good one with wires to force voltage back above the protection threshold. It can appear to work, and what it does is bypass a safety circuit that locked out for a reason, recharging cells that the electronics judged unsafe to charge. The failure mode for a damaged lithium cell is a fire that is difficult to extinguish. The saving is not worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also not worth doing: opening the pack to replace individual cells unless you genuinely know what you are doing. It voids everything, the cells are spot-welded rather than soldered for good reason, and a mistake with lithium cells is not a minor one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest position is that a pack the charger refuses after an overnight attempt at room temperature with clean contacts is finished. Lithium batteries are consumables with a service life, and this is what the end of that life looks like.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charger-fault" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When It Is the Charger, Not the Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            People assume the battery because it is the part that visibly ages, but chargers fail too and they are cheaper to replace.
          </p>

          <p>
            The clearest indication is several packs failing in a row on the same charger, particularly if they worked recently. One dead battery is a battery; three in a fortnight is a charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            No lights at all with the charger plugged into a known-working outlet points at the charger or its cord. Check the cord along its length for damage and check the plug, and if the charger has a removable cord, try another one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chemistry mismatch is worth ruling out if the charger is old. A charger made for the original blue nickel-cadmium packs lacks the circuitry lithium requires, and inserting a lithium pack will produce a fault indication or no useful charging. Look for dual chemistry marking on the label. Our guide on ONE+ battery interchangeability covers the generations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Overheating chargers throttle or stop. A charger working hard in a hot garage, or one with blocked ventilation, may pause partway through. Move it somewhere cooler with clear airflow and try again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Given that a replacement charger costs considerably less than a set of batteries, and that a faulty charger can be indicating faults on perfectly good packs, replacing a suspect charger is often the cheaper diagnostic step rather than the last resort.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Warranty, Replacement and Disposal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before buying a replacement, check whether you are owed one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi batteries carry a manufacturer warranty, and the term differs between battery types and between regions, so check the documentation for your specific pack rather than assuming. Registering tools and batteries at purchase makes any later claim considerably easier, and almost nobody does it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep proof of purchase. Warranty claims on batteries generally require it, and a receipt in a drawer is the difference between a free replacement and buying one. Photographing receipts when you buy tools is a two-second habit that pays off years later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be aware that warranties generally exclude damage from misuse, and storage damage can fall into that category. A pack that failed from deep discharge after a winter in a shed may not be covered, which is another argument for the storage habits in our battery lifespan guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            When a pack is genuinely finished, dispose of it correctly. Lithium batteries must not go in household waste: they are a fire risk in collection vehicles and at sorting facilities, and they contain recoverable materials. Most hardware retailers and municipal recycling centres accept them, often free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tape over the terminals before transporting a dead pack, particularly if it is going in a bag with anything metal. A pack that will not charge can still have enough energy to short, and a short in a bag of screws is exactly the scenario to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if a pack is swollen, distorted, unusually hot, or smells of anything chemical, treat it as urgent. Do not charge it, do not put it in a tool, keep it away from anything flammable, and take it for disposal promptly. That is the one failure mode where the right response is immediate rather than considered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a Refusal Means a Dead Battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A flashing red and green indication is usually a temperature lockout, not a fault, and it clears once the pack reaches room temperature. This is the single most common false alarm, and a large share of batteries declared dead are simply too hot from hard use or too cold from a shed or a van.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Giving Up on a Deep-Discharged Pack After Ten Minutes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many chargers attempt recovery by trickling a small current for an extended period before normal charging begins, and that can take hours with no visible progress. Leave a suspect pack on the charger overnight before concluding anything. A solid red light often means recovery is under way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Warming a Cold Battery Artificially</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Never use a radiator, an oven, direct sun or a heat gun to bring a cold pack into range, and equally never put a hot pack in a freezer. Rapid temperature change is its own stressor and the extremes are genuinely dangerous. Room temperature and one to two hours is the whole method.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Jump-Starting a Dead Pack From a Good One</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This bypasses a protection circuit that locked out because the cells fell below the voltage at which charging is safe. It can appear to work while recharging cells the electronics judged unsafe, and the failure mode for a damaged lithium cell is a fire that is hard to extinguish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the Battery When It Is the Charger</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Several packs failing in a row on one charger is a charger, not a run of bad batteries. Cross-test a known-good pack in the suspect charger and the suspect pack in another charger — a one-minute test that separates the two. A replacement charger costs far less than a set of batteries.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my Ryobi battery not charging?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In order of likelihood: it is outside the safe temperature window and the charger has locked out, it has deep-discharged below the voltage at which charging is permitted, the contacts are dirty, the charger is faulty, or the pack has reached the end of its life. Let it sit indoors at room temperature for two hours and try again first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does a flashing red light on a Ryobi charger mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally a defective pack or a fault condition, as distinct from flashing red and green, which usually indicates a temperature problem. Patterns vary slightly between charger models, so check the label or manual. Before concluding the pack is dead, clean the contacts, let it reach room temperature and leave it on overnight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you revive a dead Ryobi battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, if it is deep-discharged rather than damaged. Leave it on the charger overnight, since many chargers trickle a recovery current for hours before normal charging starts. What you should not do is jump it from another pack, which bypasses a safety lockout and can result in charging cells that are no longer safe.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a Ryobi battery take to charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies widely by charger and capacity, from around half an hour on a rapid charger with a small pack to several hours on a standard charger with a large one. A pack that suddenly charges much faster than it used to has lost capacity, which is an ageing signal rather than good news.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my charger say my battery is defective when it works in a tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often a dirty or damaged data contact. Lithium packs communicate with the charger through contacts beyond the main power terminals, so a pack can deliver power to a tool perfectly while failing to communicate with the charger. Clean all the contacts with isopropyl alcohol and try again, and cross-test on another charger.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I charge a Ryobi battery in the cold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, and the charger will prevent it. Charging lithium below freezing plates metallic lithium onto the anode, causing permanent damage and a potential safety hazard. Bring the pack indoors and let it reach room temperature over one to two hours, longer for a large pack from a genuinely cold shed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I dispose of a dead Ryobi battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in household waste, where lithium packs are a fire risk in collection vehicles and sorting facilities. Most hardware retailers and municipal recycling centres accept them, usually free. Tape over the terminals before transporting it, because a pack that will not charge can still carry enough energy to short.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

