        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Work through it in order and most problems resolve in the first two steps. Read what the charger is signalling, let the pack sit indoors at room temperature for a couple of hours, clean the contacts on both the pack and the charger, then leave it on the charger overnight in case a recovery cycle is running. Cross-test with another pack or charger if you have one.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A battery that still refuses after all of that is almost certainly finished, and that is what the end of a lithium pack's life looks like rather than a puzzle to solve. Do not jump it from a good pack to force it past the protection circuit — that lockout exists because the cells are no longer safe to charge. Recycle it properly with the terminals taped, and store the replacement at half charge somewhere indoors.
          </p>
